# Bundle / Bulk Agent Pipeline Flow

This rule owns **bundle (bulk multi-story) triggering and routing**. It is a companion to
`agent-flow.mdc` — both are always-on. Single-story / bug triggers, and all **shared
infrastructure** used by bundle agents, remain in `agent-flow.mdc` and are inherited here:

- Pack resolution (`{PACK}`) — `agent-flow.mdc § Pack Resolution`
- Bundle path variables (`$BUNDLE_*`) — `agent-flow.mdc § Procedure C — Bundle mode`
- Bundle-state file contract (`_bundle-state.yaml`) — `agent-flow.mdc § Bundle-state file contract`
- Context Pressure Detection, Tool Usage Tracking, Build report contract, MCP role resolution,
  Trigger directives — all in `agent-flow.mdc` (shared by single-story and bundle agents alike).

**Invocation rule (same as single-story):** on a matching bundle trigger you MUST (1) load the
matching `bundle-*.md` agent prompt fully, (2) execute it with real tool calls, (3) never reply
with a prose description. In Cursor prefix with `@<agent-file>.md`; Claude Code accepts the plain
trigger too.

---

## Bundle-mode triggers (dedicated agents)

Bundle-mode (multi-story) — separate dedicated agents. **Per-story execution is the default** (per-ticket gates between tickets, ONE consolidated PR). Pass `--linear` to opt out:
- `@bundle-orchestrator.md Work on epic stories {ID}, {ID}, {ID}, ...`
- `@bundle-orchestrator.md Work on epic stories {ID}, {ID}, {ID}, ... --linear` (consolidated execution — every-N checkpoints, one_per_task commits)
- `@bundle-explorer.md Run the bundle explorer`
- `@bundle-surgeon.md Run the bundle surgeon`
- `@bundle-review.md Run the bundle review`
- `@bundle-ship.md Ship the bundle`

---

## Auto-start (BUNDLE MODE — multi-story consolidation)

> 🛑 **CRITICAL — DO NOT SPLIT THE BUNDLE.** When the user issues a bundle trigger (explicit list `Work on epic stories A, B, C, D, E` or status-filter form), the **only** valid response is to dispatch ALL listed tickets, **as a single bundle**, to `bundle-orchestrator.md`. Do **NOT**:
> - Suggest dividing the list into `bundle1 / bundle2 / bundleN` choices
> - Auto-partition by layer (frontend bundle, backend bundle, ...)
> - Auto-partition by parent epic when `cross_epic_policy: warn_continue` is set
> - Drop tickets you judge "too large" or "out of scope" — that's the user's call at the C.3 gate
> - Group consecutive tickets and ask "process group A first?"
>
> If the list size exceeds `runtime.bundle.max_tickets`, the **agent itself** halts during A.1 with a clear "split or override --max=" message. The OUTER LLM (this routing layer) never proposes splits — it dispatches verbatim. The user can shrink the bundle by replying `Drop <ticket>` at the orchestrator's C.3 gate. That is the ONLY split mechanism. A pre-emptive chooser destroys the consolidated requirement summary the orchestrator builds — once the user picks one of the offered sub-bundles, the synthesis context for the unchosen tickets is lost.
>
> **Single bundle in → single bundle out.** Always.

When the user issues either of these patterns, route to **bundle-orchestrator** (NOT the regular orchestrator):

| Trigger pattern | Dispatch | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `Work on epic stories <ID>, <ID>, <ID>[, ...]` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Explicit ticket list. If `parent` field disagrees across the IDs, behavior controlled by `runtime.bundle.cross_epic_policy` (default: warn + continue). |
| `Work on epic <EPIC_ID> with status="<S1>","<S2>"[,...]` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Status-filtered child discovery. Each status must appear in `jira.status_groups` or `jira.status_map`. |
| `Work on epic <EPIC_ID> status="<S>"` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Terser form, same as above. |
| `Work on epic <EPIC_ID> group=<key>` | `bundle-orchestrator.md` | Status-group shorthand (`group=active_hydrate` etc., from `jira.status_groups`). Off when `runtime.bundle.status_filter.allow_groups: false`. |

**Disambiguation from single-story trigger:** the bundle router checks for the keyword `epic` AND either (a) two-or-more comma-separated ticket IDs after `epic stories`, or (b) the substring `with status=` / `status=` / `group=` after a single epic ID. Anything else routes to the regular orchestrator. The single-story trigger `Work on PROJ-1234` is unaffected.

**Pre-flight gates fired by `bundle-orchestrator` (in this order, before C.3):**

| Gate | When it fires | What user picks | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| BR.0 — bundle resume | `_bundle-state.yaml` already exists for the same computed `BUNDLE_ID` | `Resume` / `Resume from T<N>` / `Resume --fresh` / `Inspect state` / `Abandon bundle` | Resume / re-synth / halt |
| BR.0c — pre-existing context | ANY per-ticket file (`mode: story`/`bundle-card`) for any candidate ticket OR overlapping bundle state | `Resume <BUNDLE_ID>` / `Re-run --fresh` / `Skip implemented` / `Show <id>` / `Cancel` | Branch back to BR.0 OR set fresh flags OR proceed |
| A.1.5 — implementation evidence | After A.1 fetches tickets, BEFORE A.2 path computation | `Go` (accept SKIP/REVIEW_ONLY recommendations) / `Include all` / `Skip <ids>` / `Force <ids>` / `Show evidence <id>` / `Cancel` | Trim `{tickets}`; populate `{review_only_roster}` |
| C.3 — final gate | After Phase B writes all artifacts | `Go` / `Amend: <text>` / `Drop <ticket>` / `Show details` / `Cancel` | Branch creation (C.4) only on `Go` |

All four are `⛔ MANDATORY HALT` gates. The orchestrator must render the gate and wait for the user's reply before any subsequent step. Auto-progressing past any of these gates is a bug.

**Default execution mode: per-story.** Bundle mode runs in **per-story** execution by default (per-ticket gates, `one_per_ticket` commits, ONE PR). Set `runtime.bundle.execution_mode: consolidated` in `pipeline.yaml` to flip the project-wide default; trigger flags below override per-run.

**JIRA labels for bundle correlation (auto-applied):** Every ticket in a bundle is automatically labeled in JIRA so any single ticket clearly shows it was processed in bulk mode and which siblings it traveled with. Configurable via `runtime.bundle.jira_labels` in `pipeline.yaml`:

```yaml
runtime:
  bundle:
    jira_labels:
      enabled: true                                 # set false to disable
      prefix: "agentic_team_bulk"               # all labels start with this string
      mode: "all_tickets"                           # all_tickets | siblings | bundle_id | both
        # all_tickets (DEFAULT — matches user request format):
        #   Every ticket gets len(bundle) labels — one per bundled ticket (including itself).
        #   Example for bundle [PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432]:
        #     PROJ-1234 gets: agentic_team_bulkPROJ-1234,
        #                     agentic_team_bulkPROJ-2345,
        #                     agentic_team_bulkPROJ-3432
        #     PROJ-2345 gets the same 3 labels
        #     PROJ-3432 gets the same 3 labels
        #   JIRA query "labels = agentic_team_bulkPROJ-1234" returns ALL 3 tickets.
        # siblings:
        #   Every ticket gets len(bundle)-1 labels — one per OTHER ticket. Smaller label
        #   footprint; opening a ticket shows its siblings but not itself.
        # bundle_id:
        #   Every ticket gets ONE label of form: {prefix}{BUNDLE_ID}
        #   (e.g. agentic_team_bulkPROJ-100-BUNDLE-A3F2). Cleanest single-correlation;
        #   loses per-sibling visibility.
        # both:
        #   Combination of bundle_id + all_tickets. Most labels but maximum queryability.

      apply_at: "both"                              # orchestrator_c4 | ship_phase6 | both (default)
        # orchestrator_c4: applied right after branch creation (early visibility)
        # ship_phase6:     applied alongside JIRA transitions at end-of-bundle
        # both:            applied at C.4 AND re-applied at ship Phase 6 (idempotent;
        #                  catches cases where C.4 failed, was skipped, or labels were
        #                  manually removed mid-bundle)

      include_review_only: true                     # apply labels to {review_only_roster} tickets too
      include_skipped_by_evidence: false            # by default, do NOT label tickets that A.1.5
                                                    # marked SKIP (already-shipped tickets stay clean)

      # Idempotency: applying a label that's already present is a no-op (JIRA API). Safe to
      # re-run. Existing labels on the ticket (added by humans, other automation) are
      # preserved — labels are unioned, never overwritten.
```

**JIRA-side semantics:**
- Apply via `story_source` MCP (or `gh` / `glab` CLI fallback if vcs MCP) using the host's label-update endpoint. JIRA: `PUT /rest/api/3/issue/{key}` with `{"update":{"labels":[{"add":"<name>"}, ...]}}`.
- Labels are case-insensitive in JIRA; normalize to lowercase prefix + verbatim ticket ID.
- Failure to apply a label → record in `_bundle-state.yaml` `stages.<stage>.label_apply_failures[]` and proceed; ship Phase 6 retries non-blocking.
- Auto-applied labels are NOT removed if the bundle is cancelled or `Drop <ticket>` is used — they remain as audit trail. To remove manually: `Amend: remove jira labels` at C.3 (orchestrator-side cleanup) or via JIRA UI.

**Surfacing at gates:**
- Bundle-orchestrator C.3 gate: shows the intended label set so user sees what will be applied on `Go`.
- Bundle-ship Phase 6: shows labels alongside transitions in the per-ticket section.
- `_bundle-state.yaml` records final applied label set per ticket under `stages.ship.labels_applied[ticket][]`.

**Why this matters:** without these labels, a bundle is invisible from JIRA. Users browsing JIRA see "PROJ-1234 in In Progress" with no signal that it's part of a 5-ticket bundle being implemented together. Querying "what was bundled with PROJ-1234?" requires reading commit messages or pipeline state files. With auto-labels, a single JIRA query (`labels = agentic_team_bulkPROJ-1234`) returns the whole bundle.

**Optional flags on the bundle trigger:**

| Flag | Alias | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `--deep` | — | Phase A runs grep-based file-overlap analysis across the working tree per story (~10–20K extra tokens, amortized). Default off (set by `runtime.bundle.deep_overlap_default`). |
| `--max=<N>` | — | Override `runtime.bundle.max_tickets` for this run. Cannot exceed pipeline-config max. |
| `--fresh` | — | Same semantic as single-story `--fresh`: ignore prior `_bundle-state.yaml` and re-synthesize from JIRA. The branch is NOT deleted. |
| **`--linear`** | **`--no-each`, `--consolidated`** | **Opt out of per-story execution** (per-story is the default — see above). Reverts to legacy consolidated execution: `task_ordering: layer_dep`, every-N-tasks checkpoints in surgeon/review, `one_per_task` commit grouping in ship. Use for small bundles (≤3 tickets), dense shared code, or autonomous one-shot runs where per-ticket gates feel ceremonial. Sticky across resumes — once `flags.each: false` is in state, downstream agents respect it. |
| `--each` | `--per-story` | **No-op (default behavior).** Per-story execution is the default. Kept as a backward-compat alias; emits a non-blocking "redundant flag" notice. Mutually exclusive with `--linear` (passing both halts). |
| `--offline` / `--skip <names>` / `--only <names>` | — | Same flag family as single-story; pass through to MCP role resolution. |

> **Trigger directives** (`context:`, `reference:`, `out_of_scope:`, `constraints:`) work identically on bundle and single-story triggers — full grammar in `agent-flow.mdc § Trigger directives`.

**Quick reference:**

```
# Default — per-story execution (no flag needed):
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432
Work on epic <EPIC_ID> with status="In Progress","To Do"

# Opt out — consolidated/legacy execution:
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345, PROJ-3432 --linear

# Combined with --fresh to re-synthesize:
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345 --fresh           # default per-story, fresh
Work on epic stories PROJ-1234, PROJ-2345 --linear --fresh  # consolidated, fresh
```

Execution shape (default — per-story):

```
bundle-orchestrator   (Go at C.3)
  └─ bundle-explorer  (Go at Phase 0; runs entire bundle's exploration in one pass)
      └─ bundle-surgeon (Go at Phase 0)
          ├─ Implement SHARED prelude tasks (multi-source) → per-ticket gate (Go)
          ├─ Implement PROJ-1234's tasks               → per-ticket gate (Go)
          ├─ Implement PROJ-2345's tasks               → per-ticket gate (Go)
          ├─ Implement PROJ-3432's tasks               → end-of-stage gate
          └─ ...
              └─ bundle-review (Go at Phase 0)
                  ├─ Review SHARED tasks       → per-ticket sub-verdict gate (Go)
                  ├─ Review PROJ-1234's tasks  → per-ticket sub-verdict gate (Go)
                  ├─ Review PROJ-2345's tasks  → per-ticket sub-verdict gate (Go)
                  ├─ Review PROJ-3432's tasks  → end-of-stage gate
                  └─ bundle-ship (Go at Phase 0)
                      → commit-per-ticket (one_per_ticket, forced)
                      → ONE PR listing all tickets
                      → JIRA transitions per ticket
```

The total number of `Go` gates under `--each` with N implementing tickets is roughly:
`5 (one start-of-stage per agent) + 2N (per-ticket gates in surgeon and review) + ~3 (orchestrator pre-flight, ship verdict, ship final)`.
That's a feature, not a bug — `--each` is for users who want explicit pause-points. Use the consolidated default if you want fewer interruptions.

